# A 30-second spoken answer for each SRE-book lesson that didn't have one, built from that lesson's own takeaways.
SAY = {
    3: 'The cloud swapped physical limits for financial and logical ones, so capacity became a cost decision. I treat a provider’s SLA as a floor, not a promise about my service, and design across its failure domains. And I use SLOs alongside RTO and RPO, because those two only see up or down, while most real failures are partial.',
    4: 'SRE culture rests on two things: focus on the user, and reliability as a core value. Blameless doesn’t mean nobody is accountable. People are accountable for telling what happened so the system can be fixed, and discipline is kept for reckless acts. Culture changes through practice: learning builds confidence, confidence changes behaviour, and repeated behaviour becomes the culture.',
    5: 'I hire SREs for breadth and for strengths, because breadth predicts success in this job better than depth in one area. Training has to build confidence through practice, like shadowing on-call and running drills, and I’d run it with the same care as a production service. On-call is the main reason people leave, so I keep it flexible and compensated.',
    6: 'Most organisations end up hybrid: a central group for standards, tooling and community, and dedicated SRE teams for the most critical products. The limits I hold to are at least half of the time on engineering, at most a quarter on-call, and enough people for a humane rota, about six per site across two sites. Scorecards are for coaching teams, never for ranking them.',
    7: 'An engagement model is the contract for the work, separate from the reporting line: what SRE takes on, what the product team keeps, and how either side ends it. I wouldn’t build one around on-call alone, because on-call exists to feed engineering work. And the priority differs by customer: consumer services mitigate first, enterprise ones communicate first.',
    8: 'An SLI is good events over valid events, the SLO is the target, and the error budget is what’s left: for 99.9% over 30 days that’s 43.2 minutes. The target is a business decision made with product, written down with a policy for what happens when the budget runs out. I alert on burn rate with two windows: 14.4× over an hour, confirmed by five minutes, pages.',
    9: 'Observability means someone without tribal knowledge can test and reject hypotheses quickly during an incident, and the number I care about most is time to mitigate. I keep the alerting path fast and simple, and the analytics path complete and cheap, with one instrumentation SDK feeding both. I compare each kind of request with its own baseline, because one global threshold hides most regressions.',
    10: 'Roles first: an incident commander who directs and delegates, with operations and communications split off as it grows. Then mitigate before understanding: roll back, drain, add capacity, degrade or block. How much risk I accept in a mitigation scales with the impact, and a reasonable mitigation that fails is never blameworthy. Declaring early is cheap; you can always downgrade.',
    11: 'A good review has an independent lead, several contributing factors and no single root cause, and it records what went right as well as what went wrong. I ask why each action made sense at the time, and every action item changes the system. I measure whether we learned by whether the next similar incident was shorter.',
    12: 'Safety is a property of the whole system, so I can’t find it by checking components one at a time. STPA models who controls what, the feedback they get and what they believe, then asks of each control action: what if it’s missing, wrong, mistimed or stopped too soon? That finds hazards with no component failure. CAST applies the same model after an incident, without blame.',
    13: 'I automate for emergencies, consistency and scale, not only to save time. Automation also creates new work and erodes skills, so I keep it observable, rate-limited and reversible. Toil is manual, repetitive work with no lasting value that grows with the service. I rank it by hours, fix whole classes of it, and keep engineering above half of the team’s time.',
    14: 'Testing measures how wrong my mental model of the system is. Configuration gets the same care as code: versioned, validated in layers and rolled out gradually. A canary is an A/B comparison against a control running at the same time, judged with statistics, and too little traffic means inconclusive, not pass. For chaos tests I check that the fault really happened before calling it a pass.',
    15: 'I size for the peak at a target utilisation with one failure domain lost, so 9,000 requests a second across three zones is 195 instances, not 90. Planning for every edge case is like targeting 100%, so I pick the risk on purpose and shed low-priority traffic when it’s exceeded. Pooling saves capacity because peaks rarely coincide, as long as the pool survives a stockout.',
    16: 'Law and ethics first, then integrity, security, availability and efficiency. Replication isn’t a backup, because it copies deletions and corruption faithfully. I set the recovery point, recovery time and how long recovery must stay possible, and let those drive the design. A backup I haven’t restored is a hope, so restores are tested automatically and the restored data is validated.',
    17: 'Resilience has to work every time, so I enforce it through the platform instead of asking each team to remember. The test is overload: a service sized for X that receives 10X should keep serving about X and recover without people, which takes deadlines, load shedding by priority, retry budgets and backoff with jitter. I also practise the emergency mode, including losing chat, access and docs.',
    18: 'A good platform makes the reliable way the easy way. I prefer golden paths to mandates: make the good outcome the least effort and teams choose it. For existing systems I block new violations, give old ones an explicit exemption with an owner and an expiry date, and keep the remaining risk visible to leadership.',
    19: 'If delivery gets faster and nothing else changes, incidents rise in proportion, because change causes most of them. AI coding agents make that sharper: they produce technical debt tirelessly unless specifications keep the design coherent. So the controls have to scale with the speed: progressive rollout, automated verification and rollback. People stay the understanders of last resort, so their skills need practice.',
    20: 'For an AI system, a fast, successful response with a wrong answer is still a failure, so quality needs its own SLO next to availability and latency. I’d watch freshness and input drift, and page on “up but wrong”. Hardware, models and prompts all get qualified and canaried. Accelerators are scarce, so I’d explain idle capacity before buying more.',
    21: 'Trust in an operations agent is earned per task: read-only first, then proposing, then acting on narrow, reversible things. An agent may only do what it knows how to undo, as one validated step with evidence and an audit trail, behind a kill switch. Not every system should reach full autonomy; the level should match the risk.',
    22: 'I adapt the principles to the constraints and don’t try to copy Google. As a lone SRE I prioritise hard: stop the bleeding, get visibility, make deploys safe, then enable developers. With closed or legacy systems I stabilise what surrounds them and standardise the layer above. An SLO measured at the edge still works when I can’t change what’s inside.',
    23: 'Reliability matters more each year as more depends on software, and people stay in the loop for novel problems. The direction is to shift down: put reliability into platforms so it’s the default, not each team’s project. I’d treat risk as the leading indicator, where incidents are the lagging one, and use AI to find and remove whole classes of risk.',
}
